  "published": "2026-04-25T05:05:51.717Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v3.1.8 stable: Wire `TcpListenerService` into `WebTorrentClient` as a first-class API. Purely additive on top of 3.1.7 — no behavior change for existing consumers. New surface: `WebTorrentClient.TcpListener` property (`TcpListenerService?`); `WebTorrentClient.EnsureTcpListenerAsync(port, address)` method (idempotent, mirrors `EnsureDhtAsync`); `WebTorrentClientOptions.TcpListenPort` (`int?` — null=disabled, 0=ephemeral, >0=specific port); `WebTorrentClientOptions.TcpListenAddress` (defaults to `IPAddress.Any` so external peers can reach the listener; pass `IPAddress.Loopback` for localhost-only test harnesses). When `TcpListenPort` is set, the constructor fires `EnsureTcpListenerAsync` fire-and-forget; `client.DisposeAsync` releases the listening socket. New `Desktop_TcpListenerOption_AcceptsInboundLeech` test in PlaywrightMultiTest locks the API contract: two clients on loopback, A seeds via `TcpListenPort=0`, B leeches via direct `TcpPeer.ConnectAsync` to A's kernel-assigned port; full 64 KiB SHA-256 byte-identical round-trip in ~10s with no peer-discovery sources enabled (deterministic). Reverse-direction interop test (`qbittorrent_reverse_liveswarm.cs`) simplified to use the option. Mainline-client interop (qBittorrent, libtorrent, Transmission) unchanged - dial in by IP+port and the listener routes by info_hash to the matching torrent. See SpawnDev.WebTorrent 3.1.7 release notes for the underlying wire-level fixes that make seeding correct in the first place.",
